with bank as (
  select * from jsonb_to_recordset($questions$[{"pack_slug":"assessing-inferences","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":10,"subtype":"Inference","difficulty":"easy","stimulus":"The Marlbeck library service surveyed 2,000 residents last spring. 62% of respondents said they had borrowed a book in the previous year, 41% said they had used the free computers, and only 9% said they had attended a library event. The service is considering closing its two smallest branches, which together account for about 3% of all loans.","prompt":"Inference: \"More than half of the residents surveyed had borrowed a book in the previous year.\"\n\nHow should this inference be rated, based only on the passage?","options":["True","Probably true","Insufficient data","Probably false","False"],"correct_answer":"True","explanation":"62% of respondents borrowed a book, and 62% is more than half.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"assessing-inferences","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":11,"subtype":"Inference","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"The Marlbeck library service surveyed 2,000 residents last spring. 62% of respondents said they had borrowed a book in the previous year, 41% said they had used the free computers, and only 9% said they had attended a library event. The service is considering closing its two smallest branches, which together account for about 3% of all loans.","prompt":"Inference: \"Most of the residents who used the free computers had also borrowed a book.\"\n\nHow should this inference be rated, based only on the passage?","options":["True","Probably true","Insufficient data","Probably false","False"],"correct_answer":"Insufficient data","explanation":"The passage gives the share who borrowed and the share who used computers, but nothing about how far those groups overlap. The two could be entirely separate.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"assessing-inferences","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":12,"subtype":"Inference","difficulty":"easy","stimulus":"The Marlbeck library service surveyed 2,000 residents last spring. 62% of respondents said they had borrowed a book in the previous year, 41% said they had used the free computers, and only 9% said they had attended a library event. The service is considering closing its two smallest branches, which together account for about 3% of all loans.","prompt":"Inference: \"More residents attended a library event than used the free computers.\"\n\nHow should this inference be rated, based only on the passage?","options":["True","Probably true","Insufficient data","Probably false","False"],"correct_answer":"False","explanation":"Only 9% attended an event, against 41% who used the free computers, so the statement is the wrong way round.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"assessing-inferences","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":13,"subtype":"Inference","difficulty":"easy","stimulus":"Copperleaf Bakeries opened twelve new shops during 2023. In the same year its average daily sales per shop fell by 6%, although total sales for the company rose by 18%.","prompt":"Inference: \"Total company sales in 2023 were higher than in 2022.\"\n\nHow should this inference be rated, based only on the passage?","options":["True","Probably true","Insufficient data","Probably false","False"],"correct_answer":"True","explanation":"The passage says total sales rose by 18% in the same year.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"assessing-inferences","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":14,"subtype":"Inference","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"Copperleaf Bakeries opened twelve new shops during 2023. In the same year its average daily sales per shop fell by 6%, although total sales for the company rose by 18%.","prompt":"Inference: \"The new shops sold less per shop per day than the older shops did.\"\n\nHow should this inference be rated, based only on the passage?","options":["True","Probably true","Insufficient data","Probably false","False"],"correct_answer":"Probably true","explanation":"Average sales per shop fell in a year when twelve new shops joined, which is most easily explained by the new shops selling less. It is not certain: the older shops' own sales might have fallen instead.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"assessing-inferences","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":15,"subtype":"Inference","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"Copperleaf Bakeries opened twelve new shops during 2023. In the same year its average daily sales per shop fell by 6%, although total sales for the company rose by 18%.","prompt":"Inference: \"Each of the twelve new shops sold more per day than the average older shop did.\"\n\nHow should this inference be rated, based only on the passage?","options":["True","Probably true","Insufficient data","Probably false","False"],"correct_answer":"Probably false","explanation":"If every new shop had out-sold the older ones, the company-wide average per shop would be expected to rise, not fall. It is not impossible, so this is probably false rather than false.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"assessing-inferences","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":16,"subtype":"Inference","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"A city council counted cyclists on one commuter route on the first Tuesday of each month. In January the count was 410; by June it had risen to 890, and in December it was 380. The council notes that a new cycle lane opened in March.","prompt":"Inference: \"Cyclist numbers on this route rose in every month between January and June.\"\n\nHow should this inference be rated, based only on the passage?","options":["True","Probably true","Insufficient data","Probably false","False"],"correct_answer":"Insufficient data","explanation":"The passage gives only the January and June counts. The counts in between could have gone up and down along the way.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"assessing-inferences","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":17,"subtype":"Inference","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"A charity's helpline is staffed from 8 am to 8 pm. Last year it received 36,000 calls; 70% of them arrived between 4 pm and 8 pm. The charity's average call lasted 14 minutes, and callers who waited more than five minutes were far more likely to hang up before speaking to anyone.","prompt":"Inference: \"Fewer than half of the calls arrived before 4 pm.\"\n\nHow should this inference be rated, based only on the passage?","options":["True","Probably true","Insufficient data","Probably false","False"],"correct_answer":"True","explanation":"70% arrived between 4 pm and 8 pm, so at most 30% arrived earlier, which is fewer than half.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"recognising-assumptions","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":10,"subtype":"Recognising assumptions","difficulty":"easy","stimulus":"Statement: \"We should move the weekly team meeting to the morning — people are too tired to concentrate at four in the afternoon.\"","prompt":"Proposed assumption: \"People concentrate better in the morning than at four in the afternoon.\"\n\nIs this assumption made in the statement?","options":["Assumption made","Assumption not made"],"correct_answer":"Assumption made","explanation":"Moving the meeting only makes sense as a fix for tiredness if mornings are better for concentration. The statement takes this for granted.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"recognising-assumptions","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":11,"subtype":"Recognising assumptions","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"Statement: \"We should move the weekly team meeting to the morning — people are too tired to concentrate at four in the afternoon.\"","prompt":"Proposed assumption: \"Every member of the team is available in the morning.\"\n\nIs this assumption made in the statement?","options":["Assumption made","Assumption not made"],"correct_answer":"Assumption not made","explanation":"The statement gives a reason for moving the meeting but does not rely on everyone's availability; it does not say or presuppose anything about that.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"recognising-assumptions","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":12,"subtype":"Recognising assumptions","difficulty":"hard","stimulus":"Statement: \"The council should publish its spending online; residents are entitled to see how their money is used.\"","prompt":"Proposed assumption: \"Residents' money is the only source of the council's income.\"\n\nIs this assumption made in the statement?","options":["Assumption made","Assumption not made"],"correct_answer":"Assumption not made","explanation":"The statement says residents are entitled to see how their money is used. It does not claim, or rely on, their money being the council's only income.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"recognising-assumptions","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":13,"subtype":"Recognising assumptions","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"Statement: \"Because the shop's window display has changed, sales will rise next month.\"","prompt":"Proposed assumption: \"The window display influences how much customers buy.\"\n\nIs this assumption made in the statement?","options":["Assumption made","Assumption not made"],"correct_answer":"Assumption made","explanation":"Linking a display change to a rise in sales only works if displays affect purchasing. The statement depends on that link.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"recognising-assumptions","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":14,"subtype":"Recognising assumptions","difficulty":"easy","stimulus":"Statement: \"Our new laptop is lighter than the old one, so it will be better for people who travel.\"","prompt":"Proposed assumption: \"Travellers value a lighter laptop.\"\n\nIs this assumption made in the statement?","options":["Assumption made","Assumption not made"],"correct_answer":"Assumption made","explanation":"The argument treats lightness as a benefit for travellers; without that assumption the conclusion would not follow.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"recognising-assumptions","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":15,"subtype":"Recognising assumptions","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"Statement: \"Our new laptop is lighter than the old one, so it will be better for people who travel.\"","prompt":"Proposed assumption: \"The new laptop has a longer battery life than the old one.\"\n\nIs this assumption made in the statement?","options":["Assumption made","Assumption not made"],"correct_answer":"Assumption not made","explanation":"The statement is about weight only. Battery life is neither stated nor needed for the reasoning.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"recognising-assumptions","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":16,"subtype":"Recognising assumptions","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"Statement: \"I'll take the 7:15 train instead of the 7:45; the earlier one is always less crowded.\"","prompt":"Proposed assumption: \"A less crowded train is preferable to a more crowded one.\"\n\nIs this assumption made in the statement?","options":["Assumption made","Assumption not made"],"correct_answer":"Assumption made","explanation":"Choosing the earlier train because it is less crowded presupposes that a less crowded train is better.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"recognising-assumptions","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":17,"subtype":"Recognising assumptions","difficulty":"hard","stimulus":"Statement: \"The gallery has reduced its ticket price to £6 in the hope of attracting more visitors.\"","prompt":"Proposed assumption: \"A lower price will attract more visitors.\"\n\nIs this assumption made in the statement?","options":["Assumption made","Assumption not made"],"correct_answer":"Assumption made","explanation":"The price cut is described as aiming to increase visitors, which assumes price affects attendance.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"making-deductions","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":10,"subtype":"Deduction","difficulty":"easy","stimulus":"All the analysts in the office are on the graduate scheme.\nNo one on the graduate scheme works part-time.","prompt":"Conclusion: \"No analyst in the office works part-time.\"\n\nDoes the conclusion follow necessarily from the statements above? Treat the statements as true, even if they are not in real life.","options":["Conclusion follows","Conclusion does not follow"],"correct_answer":"Conclusion follows","explanation":"Analysts are all on the scheme, and nobody on the scheme is part-time, so no analyst can be part-time.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"making-deductions","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":11,"subtype":"Deduction","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"Some of the volunteers are teachers.\nAll teachers have been background-checked.","prompt":"Conclusion: \"Some volunteers have been background-checked.\"\n\nDoes the conclusion follow necessarily from the statements above? Treat the statements as true, even if they are not in real life.","options":["Conclusion follows","Conclusion does not follow"],"correct_answer":"Conclusion follows","explanation":"At least one volunteer is a teacher, and every teacher is checked, so at least one volunteer is checked.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"making-deductions","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":12,"subtype":"Deduction","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"All of the delivery vans are white.\nSome white vehicles are electric.","prompt":"Conclusion: \"Some of the delivery vans are electric.\"\n\nDoes the conclusion follow necessarily from the statements above? Treat the statements as true, even if they are not in real life.","options":["Conclusion follows","Conclusion does not follow"],"correct_answer":"Conclusion does not follow","explanation":"The electric white vehicles might all be something other than delivery vans. The statements leave that open.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"making-deductions","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":13,"subtype":"Deduction","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"No one who arrived after nine was given a badge.\nMeera was given a badge.","prompt":"Conclusion: \"Meera did not arrive after nine.\"\n\nDoes the conclusion follow necessarily from the statements above? Treat the statements as true, even if they are not in real life.","options":["Conclusion follows","Conclusion does not follow"],"correct_answer":"Conclusion follows","explanation":"If she had arrived after nine she would not have received a badge. She did, so she cannot have arrived after nine.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"making-deductions","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":14,"subtype":"Deduction","difficulty":"hard","stimulus":"If the server fails, the backup switches on.\nThe backup did not switch on.","prompt":"Conclusion: \"The server did not fail.\"\n\nDoes the conclusion follow necessarily from the statements above? Treat the statements as true, even if they are not in real life.","options":["Conclusion follows","Conclusion does not follow"],"correct_answer":"Conclusion follows","explanation":"The backup would have switched on if the server had failed; it did not, so the server did not fail.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"making-deductions","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":15,"subtype":"Deduction","difficulty":"hard","stimulus":"If the server fails, the backup switches on.\nThe backup switched on.","prompt":"Conclusion: \"The server failed.\"\n\nDoes the conclusion follow necessarily from the statements above? Treat the statements as true, even if they are not in real life.","options":["Conclusion follows","Conclusion does not follow"],"correct_answer":"Conclusion does not follow","explanation":"The backup might switch on for other reasons (a test, a power dip). Knowing the backup started does not tell you the server failed.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"making-deductions","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":16,"subtype":"Deduction","difficulty":"hard","stimulus":"Every course in the catalogue is either evening or weekend.\nNo weekend course costs more than £300.\nThe Pottery course costs £450.","prompt":"Conclusion: \"The Pottery course is an evening course.\"\n\nDoes the conclusion follow necessarily from the statements above? Treat the statements as true, even if they are not in real life.","options":["Conclusion follows","Conclusion does not follow"],"correct_answer":"Conclusion follows","explanation":"It costs more than £300, so it is not a weekend course; every course is evening or weekend, so it must be an evening course.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"making-deductions","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":17,"subtype":"Deduction","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"Some of the reports are late.\nAll late reports are sent to the director.","prompt":"Conclusion: \"All the reports are sent to the director.\"\n\nDoes the conclusion follow necessarily from the statements above? Treat the statements as true, even if they are not in real life.","options":["Conclusion follows","Conclusion does not follow"],"correct_answer":"Conclusion does not follow","explanation":"Only the late reports are sent to the director. The on-time reports may or may not be.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"interpreting-information","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":10,"subtype":"Interpretation","difficulty":"easy","stimulus":"A survey of 500 employees at a logistics firm found that 320 travel to work by car, 110 by bus and the rest on foot or by bicycle. Of those who drive, 40% said they would switch to a bus if the service ran every ten minutes.","prompt":"Conclusion: \"More than half of the employees surveyed travel to work by car.\"\n\nDoes the conclusion follow beyond reasonable doubt from the information in the passage?","options":["Conclusion follows","Conclusion does not follow"],"correct_answer":"Conclusion follows","explanation":"320 out of 500 is 64%, which is more than half.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"interpreting-information","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":11,"subtype":"Interpretation","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"A survey of 500 employees at a logistics firm found that 320 travel to work by car, 110 by bus and the rest on foot or by bicycle. Of those who drive, 40% said they would switch to a bus if the service ran every ten minutes.","prompt":"Conclusion: \"Fewer than 100 of the employees surveyed walk or cycle to work.\"\n\nDoes the conclusion follow beyond reasonable doubt from the information in the passage?","options":["Conclusion follows","Conclusion does not follow"],"correct_answer":"Conclusion follows","explanation":"500 − 320 − 110 = 70 walk or cycle, which is fewer than 100.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"interpreting-information","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":12,"subtype":"Interpretation","difficulty":"hard","stimulus":"A survey of 500 employees at a logistics firm found that 320 travel to work by car, 110 by bus and the rest on foot or by bicycle. Of those who drive, 40% said they would switch to a bus if the service ran every ten minutes.","prompt":"Conclusion: \"If buses ran every ten minutes, 40% of the drivers would switch to the bus.\"\n\nDoes the conclusion follow beyond reasonable doubt from the information in the passage?","options":["Conclusion follows","Conclusion does not follow"],"correct_answer":"Conclusion does not follow","explanation":"40% of drivers said they would switch. What people say they would do is not what they will certainly do, so the conclusion goes beyond what the passage establishes.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"interpreting-information","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":13,"subtype":"Interpretation","difficulty":"easy","stimulus":"Rainfall at a reservoir last year was 20% below the ten-year average. The water company reports that reservoir levels ended the year 5% below their usual level, and that it needed to buy in water on 12 days, compared with 3 days in a typical year.","prompt":"Conclusion: \"The reservoir needed more bought-in water last year than in a typical year.\"\n\nDoes the conclusion follow beyond reasonable doubt from the information in the passage?","options":["Conclusion follows","Conclusion does not follow"],"correct_answer":"Conclusion follows","explanation":"It bought in water on 12 days against 3 in a typical year, so it needed more.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"interpreting-information","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":14,"subtype":"Interpretation","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"Rainfall at a reservoir last year was 20% below the ten-year average. The water company reports that reservoir levels ended the year 5% below their usual level, and that it needed to buy in water on 12 days, compared with 3 days in a typical year.","prompt":"Conclusion: \"Low rainfall was the only reason the company had to buy in water.\"\n\nDoes the conclusion follow beyond reasonable doubt from the information in the passage?","options":["Conclusion follows","Conclusion does not follow"],"correct_answer":"Conclusion does not follow","explanation":"The passage links the two but does not say low rainfall was the only cause; demand, leaks or maintenance could also have played a part.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"interpreting-information","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":15,"subtype":"Interpretation","difficulty":"easy","stimulus":"A training company reports that of the 240 people who started its accountancy course in 2022, 180 finished it and 150 of those passed the final exam at the first attempt.","prompt":"Conclusion: \"Exactly 60 of the people who started the course did not finish it.\"\n\nDoes the conclusion follow beyond reasonable doubt from the information in the passage?","options":["Conclusion follows","Conclusion does not follow"],"correct_answer":"Conclusion follows","explanation":"240 − 180 = 60 did not finish.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"interpreting-information","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":16,"subtype":"Interpretation","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"A training company reports that of the 240 people who started its accountancy course in 2022, 180 finished it and 150 of those passed the final exam at the first attempt.","prompt":"Conclusion: \"Most people who started the course passed the final exam at the first attempt.\"\n\nDoes the conclusion follow beyond reasonable doubt from the information in the passage?","options":["Conclusion follows","Conclusion does not follow"],"correct_answer":"Conclusion follows","explanation":"150 out of 240 is 62.5%, which is a majority of those who started.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"interpreting-information","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":17,"subtype":"Interpretation","difficulty":"hard","stimulus":"A training company reports that of the 240 people who started its accountancy course in 2022, 180 finished it and 150 of those passed the final exam at the first attempt.","prompt":"Conclusion: \"The 30 people who finished the course but did not pass at the first attempt all failed the exam.\"\n\nDoes the conclusion follow beyond reasonable doubt from the information in the passage?","options":["Conclusion follows","Conclusion does not follow"],"correct_answer":"Conclusion does not follow","explanation":"They did not pass at the first attempt, but the passage does not say whether they failed, missed the exam, or were still waiting to be assessed.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"evaluating-arguments","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":10,"subtype":"Evaluating arguments","difficulty":"easy","stimulus":"Question: Should the company introduce a four-day working week?","prompt":"Argument: \"Yes — trials at similar companies have shown that output stayed the same while staff sickness absence fell.\"\n\nIs this a strong or a weak argument? A strong argument is both important and directly related to the question; treat every argument as true.","options":["Strong argument","Weak argument"],"correct_answer":"Strong argument","explanation":"It is directly about the effect of a four-day week on the things a company cares about (output and absence), and it is a significant benefit.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"evaluating-arguments","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":11,"subtype":"Evaluating arguments","difficulty":"easy","stimulus":"Question: Should the company introduce a four-day working week?","prompt":"Argument: \"No — some people enjoy Mondays.\"\n\nIs this a strong or a weak argument? A strong argument is both important and directly related to the question; treat every argument as true.","options":["Strong argument","Weak argument"],"correct_answer":"Weak argument","explanation":"It is trivial and not directly about the working of the company; personal preference for a day of the week is not a serious reason.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"evaluating-arguments","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":12,"subtype":"Evaluating arguments","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"Question: Should all new employees be required to complete a fire-safety course in their first week?","prompt":"Argument: \"Yes — a person who does not know the evacuation routes puts themselves and others at risk in an emergency.\"\n\nIs this a strong or a weak argument? A strong argument is both important and directly related to the question; treat every argument as true.","options":["Strong argument","Weak argument"],"correct_answer":"Strong argument","explanation":"It is directly related to the purpose of the course and concerns a serious consequence: safety in an emergency.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"evaluating-arguments","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":13,"subtype":"Evaluating arguments","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"Question: Should all new employees be required to complete a fire-safety course in their first week?","prompt":"Argument: \"No — the course would take up time that new starters could use to meet their colleagues.\"\n\nIs this a strong or a weak argument? A strong argument is both important and directly related to the question; treat every argument as true.","options":["Strong argument","Weak argument"],"correct_answer":"Weak argument","explanation":"It is a minor inconvenience set against a safety issue, and the course does not stop them meeting colleagues at other times.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"evaluating-arguments","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":14,"subtype":"Evaluating arguments","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"Question: Should the local council close the swimming pool to save money?","prompt":"Argument: \"No — it is the only pool in the area, and many schools depend on it to teach children to swim.\"\n\nIs this a strong or a weak argument? A strong argument is both important and directly related to the question; treat every argument as true.","options":["Strong argument","Weak argument"],"correct_answer":"Strong argument","explanation":"It concerns a substantial and direct consequence (children losing access to swimming lessons) for a large group.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"evaluating-arguments","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":15,"subtype":"Evaluating arguments","difficulty":"hard","stimulus":"Question: Should the local council close the swimming pool to save money?","prompt":"Argument: \"Yes — the council has lots of other buildings that do not lose money.\"\n\nIs this a strong or a weak argument? A strong argument is both important and directly related to the question; treat every argument as true.","options":["Strong argument","Weak argument"],"correct_answer":"Weak argument","explanation":"The profitability of other buildings has no bearing on whether this pool should close; it is not a direct reason.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"evaluating-arguments","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":16,"subtype":"Evaluating arguments","difficulty":"hard","stimulus":"Question: Should the university ban laptops in lectures?","prompt":"Argument: \"Yes — studies of the university's own students show that those who take handwritten notes score higher on average in end-of-year exams.\"\n\nIs this a strong or a weak argument? A strong argument is both important and directly related to the question; treat every argument as true.","options":["Strong argument","Weak argument"],"correct_answer":"Strong argument","explanation":"It is direct evidence about learning outcomes at this university, which is the main purpose of a lecture.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"evaluating-arguments","type_slug":"critical-thinking","display_order":17,"subtype":"Evaluating arguments","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"Question: Should the university ban laptops in lectures?","prompt":"Argument: \"No — laptops are expensive and many students would be annoyed at leaving them at home.\"\n\nIs this a strong or a weak argument? A strong argument is both important and directly related to the question; treat every argument as true.","options":["Strong argument","Weak argument"],"correct_answer":"Weak argument","explanation":"Cost and annoyance are not directly about whether a ban would improve lectures; the reason offered does not address the educational purpose.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"graduate-programme-scenarios","type_slug":"situational-judgement","display_order":10,"subtype":"Teamwork","difficulty":"easy","stimulus":"You share a project with two colleagues. In the team chat one of them has not replied to any messages for three days, and a shared document you all need is still missing their section.","prompt":"Which response is the most effective?","options":["Finish their section yourself without telling anyone, so that the team meets the deadline and nobody is embarrassed.","Wait until the deadline and see whether their part arrives, since chasing colleagues can feel like nagging.","Message them directly and ask whether they are stuck or short of time, offering to talk it through.","Tell your manager in the team chat that they are not pulling their weight, so that it is on record early."],"correct_answer":"Message them directly and ask whether they are stuck or short of time, offering to talk it through.","explanation":"A direct, friendly check finds out what is actually wrong and keeps the working relationship intact. Quietly doing their work hides the problem; reporting them in public escalates before you have spoken to them; waiting risks the deadline.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"graduate-programme-scenarios","type_slug":"situational-judgement","display_order":11,"subtype":"Prioritisation","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"It is Thursday afternoon. Your manager has asked for a summary of last month's figures by Friday noon. This morning a senior colleague from another team asked you for help with a small data check that would take about two hours, 'whenever you can'.","prompt":"Which response is the most effective?","options":["Ask your manager to explain to the colleague that you have a deadline and cannot take on anything else.","Say you are far too busy at the moment, without offering a time or any other way forward.","Do the two-hour check first because they are senior, and finish the summary in the evening if you must.","Tell the colleague when you can help (after Friday noon), and start your summary now."],"correct_answer":"Tell the colleague when you can help (after Friday noon), and start your summary now.","explanation":"Being clear about when you can help, and protecting a committed deadline, is honest and useful to both. Putting the request first risks the deadline; a bare refusal is unhelpful; passing the message through your manager is unnecessary.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"graduate-programme-scenarios","type_slug":"situational-judgement","display_order":12,"subtype":"Communication","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"You have just presented your analysis to a client. Afterwards you realise you quoted one figure as 14% when the correct figure is 11%. The client has not noticed and the meeting is over.","prompt":"Which response is the most effective?","options":["Leave it: the client did not notice and the overall conclusions are broadly the same either way.","Email the client promptly with the corrected figure and its effect, and tell your manager what happened.","Quietly put the correct figure into the final report, without drawing attention to the earlier version.","Mention the correction at the next scheduled meeting, when it can be covered along with other business."],"correct_answer":"Email the client promptly with the corrected figure and its effect, and tell your manager what happened.","explanation":"Correcting a known error quickly and openly protects the client's decisions and your credibility. Saying nothing or slipping in a quiet change leaves the client relying on a wrong number in the meantime.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"graduate-programme-scenarios","type_slug":"situational-judgement","display_order":13,"subtype":"Initiative","difficulty":"easy","stimulus":"You finish your assigned tasks for the week on Wednesday. Your team has a backlog of small, unassigned tasks, and your manager is out of the office until Friday.","prompt":"Which response is the most effective?","options":["Use the quiet time to relax and wait, since your manager will assign more work when they are back.","Start a large piece of work that you think would be valuable, without checking that it is wanted.","Go round the team asking whether anyone needs anything, and leave it there if nobody does.","Take on a small unassigned task within your ability and tell your manager what you did."],"correct_answer":"Take on a small unassigned task within your ability and tell your manager what you did.","explanation":"Using spare time on something useful and within your remit, then reporting it, shows initiative without overstepping. Starting something large unasked, or waiting, are the two extremes.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"graduate-programme-scenarios","type_slug":"situational-judgement","display_order":14,"subtype":"Resilience","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"In your first performance review your manager says your written reports are 'too long and hard to follow'. You spent a lot of time on the last one and feel discouraged.","prompt":"Which response is the most effective?","options":["Explain that the reports were long because the subjects were complicated, and leave it at that for now.","Ask to be moved off report-writing so that you can concentrate on the tasks you are better at.","Say nothing in the meeting, and privately conclude that the feedback is unfair and not worth acting on.","Ask for specific examples of what worked less well and try those changes on your next report."],"correct_answer":"Ask for specific examples of what worked less well and try those changes on your next report.","explanation":"Turning general criticism into specific, testable changes is the most useful way to improve and shows you can take feedback. Defending, sulking or avoiding the work does not.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"graduate-programme-scenarios","type_slug":"situational-judgement","display_order":15,"subtype":"Integrity","difficulty":"hard","stimulus":"A supplier offers you a personal discount on a laptop as 'a thank-you' for choosing their company for a team purchase. Your organisation's policy on gifts is not one you have read.","prompt":"Which response is the most effective?","options":["Accept it and keep quiet about it, because it is only a small saving that nobody else would care about.","Accept it, since the team purchase has already been agreed and the discount does not change the price paid.","Accept it but suggest the supplier offers the same discount to your colleagues to make it fair.","Decline for now, read the policy, and check with your manager or compliance before deciding."],"correct_answer":"Decline for now, read the policy, and check with your manager or compliance before deciding.","explanation":"You cannot know the discount is permitted until you have checked, and it could look like an inducement linked to a business decision. Declining until you know is the safe, transparent choice.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"graduate-programme-scenarios","type_slug":"situational-judgement","display_order":16,"subtype":"Teamwork","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"In a group meeting a colleague presents an idea you think has a serious flaw. Your manager seems pleased with it.","prompt":"Which response is the most effective?","options":["Say nothing during the meeting to avoid awkwardness, and raise the concern with your manager afterwards.","Wait to see whether anyone else spots the problem, since it may not be yours to point out.","Tell the group plainly that the idea will not work, then explain in detail exactly where it fails.","Say what works, then raise the specific concern politely and suggest how to test or fix it."],"correct_answer":"Say what works, then raise the specific concern politely and suggest how to test or fix it.","explanation":"Raising a real concern constructively, in the meeting where it can be fixed, helps the team. Silence lets the flaw through; blunt dismissal harms the relationship without offering a way forward.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"graduate-programme-scenarios","type_slug":"situational-judgement","display_order":17,"subtype":"Prioritisation","difficulty":"hard","stimulus":"You have three tasks due today: a routine report your manager will look at next week, a request from a client who needs an answer by 3 pm, and an internal survey response that closes at 5 pm. It is 1 pm and each task would take about an hour.","prompt":"Which response is the most effective?","options":["Start with the routine report, because it is the largest piece of work and will take the most time.","Do the survey first because it is the quickest, then work on the client's answer with what time is left.","Ask your manager which to do first and wait for their reply before starting any of them.","Do the client's answer, then the survey, and defer the routine report to tomorrow, telling your manager."],"correct_answer":"Do the client's answer, then the survey, and defer the routine report to tomorrow, telling your manager.","explanation":"The client's 3 pm deadline is the most urgent and most consequential, and the survey has the next-nearest hard deadline. The report can move without harm if you say so. Waiting for instructions wastes the time you have.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"customer-and-stakeholder-scenarios","type_slug":"situational-judgement","display_order":10,"subtype":"Customer focus","difficulty":"easy","stimulus":"A customer phones to complain that their delivery has arrived damaged. You are not responsible for deliveries, and the delivery team has already left for the day.","prompt":"Which response is the most effective?","options":["Promise a replacement straight away, even though you are not authorised to promise one, to calm them down.","Ask the customer to phone back tomorrow when the delivery team are in the office and can help.","Explain that deliveries are not your department, and give them the general company number to try.","Apologise, take the details, say what happens next, and hand the case to the delivery team with everything they need."],"correct_answer":"Apologise, take the details, say what happens next, and hand the case to the delivery team with everything they need.","explanation":"The customer should not have to repeat themselves or wait for the right person. Owning the hand-over and telling them what happens next is helpful without promising something you cannot deliver.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"customer-and-stakeholder-scenarios","type_slug":"situational-judgement","display_order":11,"subtype":"Communication","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"A client emails you at 6 pm asking for a report by 9 am tomorrow. You know it cannot be done to a good standard by then.","prompt":"Which response is the most effective?","options":["Leave the email until the morning, when you can explain properly what was and was not possible overnight.","Agree to the deadline and work late to produce what you can, without mentioning that it may be incomplete.","Reply that the request is unreasonable and that you cannot help with a deadline this short.","Reply now with what you can deliver by 9 am, or when the full report will be ready, and ask if that works."],"correct_answer":"Reply now with what you can deliver by 9 am, or when the full report will be ready, and ask if that works.","explanation":"A prompt, honest reply with a realistic offer lets the client plan. Promising more than you can do, going silent, or refusing outright all leave them worse informed.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"customer-and-stakeholder-scenarios","type_slug":"situational-judgement","display_order":12,"subtype":"Stakeholder management","difficulty":"hard","stimulus":"You are leading a small project. Two stakeholders give you conflicting requirements for the same deliverable, and each says their view is the priority.","prompt":"Which response is the most effective?","options":["Choose the requirement that is easier to deliver and get on with it without troubling either of them.","Set the two requirements side by side, explain the trade-offs to both, and ask them to agree priorities.","Build something that partly satisfies each of them so that neither feels that their request was ignored.","Follow whichever the more senior stakeholder asked for, as that is the safest way to avoid criticism."],"correct_answer":"Set the two requirements side by side, explain the trade-offs to both, and ask them to agree priorities.","explanation":"Conflicting requirements need a decision by the people with authority to make it. Making the conflict visible and getting agreement avoids rework and resentment; guessing, splitting the difference or hiding the choice does not.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"customer-and-stakeholder-scenarios","type_slug":"situational-judgement","display_order":13,"subtype":"Customer focus","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"A regular customer is angry because the product they ordered has been out of stock for a week. They say they will take their business elsewhere.","prompt":"Which response is the most effective?","options":["Say you will pass the complaint on to the right team, and finish the call as soon as you politely can.","Offer a large discount at once, without checking whether you are allowed to, to keep them as a customer.","Listen, acknowledge the frustration, give an honest date (or promise to find one), and offer what you can.","Explain that the delay is the supplier's fault, not the company's, so the customer understands it is not being ignored."],"correct_answer":"Listen, acknowledge the frustration, give an honest date (or promise to find one), and offer what you can.","explanation":"People want to be heard and to know what will happen. Being honest about the date and offering what is within your authority is the best you can do; blaming the supplier or over-promising are not.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"customer-and-stakeholder-scenarios","type_slug":"situational-judgement","display_order":14,"subtype":"Communication","difficulty":"easy","stimulus":"You realise mid-conversation that a customer has misunderstood the terms of a warranty and thinks it covers accidental damage, which it does not.","prompt":"Which response is the most effective?","options":["Politely correct it now and explain what is and is not covered.","Let it go, because correcting them might lead to an argument that you would rather avoid.","Tell them warranties can be complicated and that they should read the full terms themselves.","Wait until they actually make a claim and explain the position then, when it becomes relevant."],"correct_answer":"Politely correct it now and explain what is and is not covered.","explanation":"Being straightforward before they rely on a wrong belief saves them cost and disappointment. Telling them later, or being vague, leaves the problem in place.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"customer-and-stakeholder-scenarios","type_slug":"situational-judgement","display_order":15,"subtype":"Stakeholder management","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"Your project is running a week behind schedule because of a delay outside your control. The sponsor is expecting a status update on Monday.","prompt":"Which response is the most effective?","options":["Tell the sponsor the project is on track but 'flexible', so that they are reassured without alarm.","Tell the sponsor before Monday, with the cause and a revised plan with options.","Report the delay on Monday as though it had only just come to light, to keep the meeting positive.","Say nothing in the hope of catching up over the weekend, so that there is nothing to report on Monday."],"correct_answer":"Tell the sponsor before Monday, with the cause and a revised plan with options.","explanation":"Bad news early, with a plan, lets the sponsor help and keeps their trust. Hiding it or dressing it up damages trust much more than the delay does.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"customer-and-stakeholder-scenarios","type_slug":"situational-judgement","display_order":16,"subtype":"Customer focus","difficulty":"hard","stimulus":"A customer asks for a refund on an item outside the 30-day refund period. It is a fair request in the circumstances (they were ill), but the policy is clear, and you are permitted to escalate exceptions to your team leader.","prompt":"Which response is the most effective?","options":["Give the refund yourself, bending the policy, since it is obviously the fair thing to do in the circumstances.","Explain the policy, say the case seems reasonable, and escalate it to your team leader while keeping the customer informed.","Tell the customer that the refund has been approved so that they stop asking, and hope it is approved.","Refuse the refund, because the policy is clear that it applies only within 30 days of purchase."],"correct_answer":"Explain the policy, say the case seems reasonable, and escalate it to your team leader while keeping the customer informed.","explanation":"Using the exception route respects the policy and the customer. Rigid refusal ignores a fair case; unilateral bending of rules or telling them something untrue undermines both.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"customer-and-stakeholder-scenarios","type_slug":"situational-judgement","display_order":17,"subtype":"Teamwork","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"You have been asked to hand over a project to a colleague who is taking it on. You are moving to another team and are busy with the new role.","prompt":"Which response is the most effective?","options":["Tell them to ask the client directly about anything that is unclear, since the client knows the project.","Send the project folder with a message saying that you are busy in the new role but will help if you can.","Hand it over in a conversation, and leave it to your colleague to write down whatever they need.","Write a clear hand-over note, talk it through with your colleague, and stay reachable for questions for a set time."],"correct_answer":"Write a clear hand-over note, talk it through with your colleague, and stay reachable for questions for a set time.","explanation":"A good hand-over is a professional responsibility; a folder and a message leave the new owner guessing, and asking the client exposes the gaps.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"integrity-and-pressure-scenarios","type_slug":"situational-judgement","display_order":10,"subtype":"Integrity","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"Your manager asks you to backdate a signature on a form so that it appears to have been completed before a deadline that was missed by two days.","prompt":"Which response is the most effective?","options":["Refuse, then report your manager to HR straight away without first discussing your concern with them.","Backdate it as asked, because your manager is responsible for the form and will have thought it through.","Backdate it, but keep a private note of what actually happened in case anyone asks in the future.","Decline to backdate it, offer to record the real date, and help to explain the delay."],"correct_answer":"Decline to backdate it, offer to record the real date, and help to explain the delay.","explanation":"Falsifying a record is dishonest whoever asks. Declining, explaining and offering an honest alternative is proportionate; reporting straight away without talking first is a step too far unless the pressure continues.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"integrity-and-pressure-scenarios","type_slug":"situational-judgement","display_order":11,"subtype":"Integrity","difficulty":"hard","stimulus":"You discover that a colleague has been claiming expenses for taxi journeys that appear to be personal. You have no direct proof, only a pattern you have noticed.","prompt":"Which response is the most effective?","options":["Take the facts you have to your manager or the confidential reporting route and leave the checking to them.","Ignore it: without proof it is not your responsibility, and getting involved could cause trouble.","Confront your colleague directly and insist that they repay whatever they should not have claimed.","Mention your suspicions to other colleagues to find out whether any of them has noticed the same thing."],"correct_answer":"Take the facts you have to your manager or the confidential reporting route and leave the checking to them.","explanation":"Reporting factual concerns through the right route protects the organisation and the colleague's right to a fair process. Confronting, ignoring or gossiping does not.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"integrity-and-pressure-scenarios","type_slug":"situational-judgement","display_order":12,"subtype":"Resilience","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"You have made a mistake that has delayed an important release by a day. Your team is under pressure and some are irritated.","prompt":"Which response is the most effective?","options":["Point out that the instructions you were given were unclear and so the delay is partly not your fault.","Work through the night to catch up without telling anyone, so that nobody has to know what went wrong.","Tell the team what happened, apologise briefly, fix it fast, and then work out how to prevent it.","Say nothing and hope that nobody traces the delay back to you before the release is finally out."],"correct_answer":"Tell the team what happened, apologise briefly, fix it fast, and then work out how to prevent it.","explanation":"Owning a mistake calmly and moving quickly to a fix keeps trust. Blaming, hiding and silent heroics leave the team in the dark.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"integrity-and-pressure-scenarios","type_slug":"situational-judgement","display_order":13,"subtype":"Prioritisation","difficulty":"hard","stimulus":"Late on Friday you are asked by two different managers to do urgent work over the weekend. Each says theirs is the most important. You can only do one.","prompt":"Which response is the most effective?","options":["Tell both managers about the clash now and ask them, or your line manager, to decide the priority.","Do whichever request arrived first and tell the other manager politely that you are not available.","Do neither, since you cannot satisfy both managers and it would not be fair to favour one of them.","Agree to both and try to do each of them quickly, accepting that neither will be as good as it could be."],"correct_answer":"Tell both managers about the clash now and ask them, or your line manager, to decide the priority.","explanation":"The conflict is for the managers to resolve with full information. Silently choosing or over-promising helps no one.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"integrity-and-pressure-scenarios","type_slug":"situational-judgement","display_order":14,"subtype":"Integrity","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"During a job assessment day another candidate leaves their notes on the table, and you notice they contain the answers to the exercise you are about to do.","prompt":"Which response is the most effective?","options":["Glance through them, since you have already seen what they are and cannot un-see it now.","Leave them unread, tell an assessor what happened, and carry on with the exercise honestly.","Take a quick photo in case you need to show somebody afterwards what you saw on the table.","Tuck the notes safely away so that the other candidate does not lose them, and say nothing about them."],"correct_answer":"Leave them unread, tell an assessor what happened, and carry on with the exercise honestly.","explanation":"Using the answers would be cheating; telling the assessors is the honest and proportionate step and protects you if anyone later asks.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"integrity-and-pressure-scenarios","type_slug":"situational-judgement","display_order":15,"subtype":"Resilience","difficulty":"easy","stimulus":"You are rejected after the final round of an interview for a job you badly wanted, and you feel embarrassed.","prompt":"Which response is the most effective?","options":["Ask politely for feedback, note what you can learn from it, and keep applying.","Decide that interviews are not your strength and that you should stop applying for jobs like this one.","Avoid telling anyone about the outcome, since it is embarrassing and will only remind you of it.","Reply to the rejection with an email setting out why you think the decision was wrong and unfair."],"correct_answer":"Ask politely for feedback, note what you can learn from it, and keep applying.","explanation":"Feedback turns a disappointment into something usable. Giving up or reacting angrily closes doors.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"integrity-and-pressure-scenarios","type_slug":"situational-judgement","display_order":16,"subtype":"Communication","difficulty":"hard","stimulus":"A colleague has told you in confidence that they are looking for another job. Your manager asks you directly whether anyone on the team is thinking of leaving.","prompt":"Which response is the most effective?","options":["Tell your manager what your colleague said, because you were asked a direct question and should answer honestly.","Say that nobody is thinking of leaving, even though you know that this is not the case, to protect them.","Warn your colleague that your manager has been asking, and share what was said so that they are prepared.","Say you cannot speak for others, and suggest your manager talks with the team about their plans."],"correct_answer":"Say you cannot speak for others, and suggest your manager talks with the team about their plans.","explanation":"You can decline to share a confidence without lying. Telling a direct falsehood, or breaking the confidence, are both worse.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"integrity-and-pressure-scenarios","type_slug":"situational-judgement","display_order":17,"subtype":"Initiative","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":"You notice that a process your team uses often causes small errors that need correcting later. No one has raised it.","prompt":"Which response is the most effective?","options":["Change the process yourself without telling anyone, since the improvement is clearly worth having.","Assume that someone more experienced has already thought about it and that a change would be unwelcome.","Collect a few examples, propose a specific improvement, and raise it with your manager or team.","Complain about the process to colleagues over lunch, since someone influential may then hear about it."],"correct_answer":"Collect a few examples, propose a specific improvement, and raise it with your manager or team.","explanation":"Evidence plus a concrete suggestion, raised through the proper route, is how an improvement actually gets adopted.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"data-interpretation","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":10,"subtype":"Table reading","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":null,"prompt":"What percentage of the total revenue came from the North region? (Give the nearest whole percent.)","options":["14%","21%","17%","15%"],"correct_answer":"17%","explanation":"North earned 150 of 870: 150 ÷ 870 × 100 = 17.2%, which is 17% to the nearest whole percent.","exhibit":{"kind":"table","caption":"Marlowe Energy: revenue by region, last year (£ thousand)","headers":["Region","Revenue (£000)"],"rows":[["North","150"],["Central","150"],["East","330"],["West","240"],["Total","870"]]},"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"data-interpretation","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":11,"subtype":"Table reading","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":null,"prompt":"What percentage of the total revenue came from the West region? (Give the nearest whole percent.)","options":["24%","21%","31%","28%"],"correct_answer":"24%","explanation":"West earned 270 of 1140: 270 ÷ 1140 × 100 = 23.7%, which is 24% to the nearest whole percent.","exhibit":{"kind":"table","caption":"Brightwater Foods: revenue by region, last year (£ thousand)","headers":["Region","Revenue (£000)"],"rows":[["North","330"],["Central","210"],["East","330"],["West","270"],["Total","1140"]]},"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"data-interpretation","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":12,"subtype":"Table reading","difficulty":"easy","stimulus":null,"prompt":"How much more revenue (in £ thousand) did the South region earn than the Central region?","options":["£120k","£180k","£510k","£150k"],"correct_answer":"£150k","explanation":"Subtract the smaller figure from the larger: 330 − 180 = 150 (£150k).","exhibit":{"kind":"table","caption":"Brightwater Foods: revenue by region, last year (£ thousand)","headers":["Region","Revenue (£000)"],"rows":[["North","120"],["Central","180"],["East","120"],["South","330"],["Total","750"]]},"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"data-interpretation","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":13,"subtype":"Table reading","difficulty":"easy","stimulus":null,"prompt":"How much more revenue (in £ thousand) did the East region earn than the North region?","options":["£60k","£90k","£30k","£600k"],"correct_answer":"£60k","explanation":"Subtract the smaller figure from the larger: 330 − 270 = 60 (£60k).","exhibit":{"kind":"table","caption":"Marlowe Energy: revenue by region, last year (£ thousand)","headers":["Region","Revenue (£000)"],"rows":[["West","330"],["East","330"],["South","210"],["North","270"],["Total","1140"]]},"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"data-interpretation","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":14,"subtype":"Profit and margin","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":null,"prompt":"What was the total profit across the four quarters?","options":["£249k","£480k","£144k","£107.25k"],"correct_answer":"£144k","explanation":"Total revenue is 480 and total costs are 336, so profit is £144k.","exhibit":{"kind":"table","caption":"Marlowe Energy: quarterly results (£ thousand)","headers":["Quarter","Revenue","Costs"],"rows":[["Q1","105","73.5"],["Q2","115","80.5"],["Q3","130","91"],["Q4","130","91"]]},"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"data-interpretation","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":15,"subtype":"Profit and margin","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":null,"prompt":"What was the profit margin (profit as a percentage of revenue) in Q2?","options":["80%","25%","33.3%","30%"],"correct_answer":"25%","explanation":"Profit is 140 − 105 = 35. Margin is profit ÷ revenue: 35 ÷ 140 × 100 = 25%. Dividing by costs (33.3%) gives mark-up, not margin.","exhibit":{"kind":"table","caption":"Ironbridge Fabrication: quarterly results (£ thousand)","headers":["Quarter","Revenue","Costs"],"rows":[["Q1","120","90"],["Q2","140","105"],["Q3","160","120"],["Q4","180","135"]]},"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"data-interpretation","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":16,"subtype":"Profit and margin","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":null,"prompt":"What was the profit margin (profit as a percentage of revenue) in Q3?","options":["30%","42.9%","35%","75%"],"correct_answer":"30%","explanation":"Profit is 140 − 98 = 42. Margin is profit ÷ revenue: 42 ÷ 140 × 100 = 30%. Dividing by costs (42.9%) gives mark-up, not margin.","exhibit":{"kind":"table","caption":"Norvale Logistics: quarterly results (£ thousand)","headers":["Quarter","Revenue","Costs"],"rows":[["Q1","100","70"],["Q2","125","87.5"],["Q3","140","98"],["Q4","135","94.5"]]},"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"data-interpretation","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":17,"subtype":"Chart reading","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":null,"prompt":"Which product had the largest increase in units sold between the two years, and by how many thousand?","options":["Bags: +12","Bottles: +96","Bottles: +16","Helmets: +5"],"correct_answer":"Bottles: +16","explanation":"The change for each product is (this year − last year): Locks -5, Lights -8, Bottles +16, Helmets +5, Bags +12. The largest rise is Bottles, +16 thousand.","exhibit":{"kind":"bar_chart","caption":"Lindqvist Textiles: units sold by product (thousands)","unit":"Units sold (thousands)","categories":["Locks","Lights","Bottles","Helmets","Bags"],"series":[{"name":"Last year","values":[50,40,80,20,60]},{"name":"This year","values":[45,32,96,25,72]}]},"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"data-interpretation","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":18,"subtype":"Chart reading","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":null,"prompt":"What was the percentage change in helmets sales from last year to this year?","options":["+10%","-20%","-11.1%","-10%"],"correct_answer":"-10%","explanation":"Helmets moved from 40 to 36, a change of -4. Divided by last year's 40 that is -10%. Dividing by this year's figure is the usual mistake (-11.1%).","exhibit":{"kind":"bar_chart","caption":"Ashdown Retail: units sold by product (thousands)","unit":"Units sold (thousands)","categories":["Bags","Helmets","Bottles","Bikes","Locks"],"series":[{"name":"Last year","values":[30,40,60,30,40]},{"name":"This year","values":[45,36,48,27,60]}]},"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"percentages-and-change","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":10,"subtype":"Percentages","difficulty":"easy","stimulus":null,"prompt":"Marlowe Energy had sales of £64,000 last year. 35% of those sales were made online. How much were the online sales?","options":["£1,828.57","£22,400","£2,240","£41,600"],"correct_answer":"£22,400","explanation":"35% of £64,000 is £64,000 × 35 ÷ 100 = £22,400. £41,600 is what is left after taking the online sales away, not the online sales themselves.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"percentages-and-change","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":11,"subtype":"Percentages","difficulty":"easy","stimulus":null,"prompt":"Norvale Logistics had sales of £36,000 last year. 65% of those sales were made online. How much were the online sales?","options":["£23,400","£2,340","£553.85","£12,600"],"correct_answer":"£23,400","explanation":"65% of £36,000 is £36,000 × 65 ÷ 100 = £23,400. £12,600 is what is left after taking the online sales away, not the online sales themselves.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"percentages-and-change","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":12,"subtype":"Percentage change","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":null,"prompt":"At Ashdown Retail, monthly sales (thousand units) rose from 160 to 176. What was the percentage increase?","options":["9.1%","10%","5%","16%"],"correct_answer":"10%","explanation":"The change is 16, and percentage change is measured against the starting figure: 16 ÷ 160 × 100 = 10%. Dividing by the new figure gives 9.1%, which is the common slip.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"percentages-and-change","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":13,"subtype":"Percentage change","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":null,"prompt":"At Brightwater Foods, staff numbers rose from 120 to 150. What was the percentage increase?","options":["25%","20%","30%","12.5%"],"correct_answer":"25%","explanation":"The change is 30, and percentage change is measured against the starting figure: 30 ÷ 120 × 100 = 25%. Dividing by the new figure gives 20%, which is the common slip.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"percentages-and-change","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":14,"subtype":"Percentages","difficulty":"hard","stimulus":null,"prompt":"Garden tools are on sale with 30% off. The sale price is £84. What was the price before the discount?","options":["£54","£120","£109.20","£67.20"],"correct_answer":"£120","explanation":"The sale price is 70% of the original, so divide by 0.7: £84 ÷ 0.7 = £120. Adding 30% back onto the sale price gives £109.20, which is wrong because the 30% was taken off the larger, original figure.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"percentages-and-change","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":15,"subtype":"Percentages","difficulty":"hard","stimulus":null,"prompt":"Solar panels are on sale with 30% off. The sale price is £175. What was the price before the discount?","options":["£140","£250","£145","£227.50"],"correct_answer":"£250","explanation":"The sale price is 70% of the original, so divide by 0.7: £175 ÷ 0.7 = £250. Adding 30% back onto the sale price gives £227.50, which is wrong because the 30% was taken off the larger, original figure.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"percentages-and-change","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":16,"subtype":"Percentage change","difficulty":"hard","stimulus":null,"prompt":"The share price of Norvale Logistics rose by 20% in one month and then fell by 25% the next. Overall, what was the change over the two months?","options":["a decrease of 25%","an increase of 45%","a decrease of 5%","a decrease of 10%"],"correct_answer":"a decrease of 10%","explanation":"Treat the start as 100. After the 20% rise it is 120; a 25% fall from that is 120 × 0.75 = 90. The overall change is a decrease of 10%. Simply subtracting the percentages (20 − 25 = -5) is wrong because the second percentage applies to a different base.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"percentages-and-change","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":17,"subtype":"Percentage change","difficulty":"hard","stimulus":null,"prompt":"The share price of Marlowe Energy rose by 25% in one month and then fell by 40% the next. Overall, what was the change over the two months?","options":["a decrease of 40%","a decrease of 25%","an increase of 65%","a decrease of 15%"],"correct_answer":"a decrease of 25%","explanation":"Treat the start as 100. After the 25% rise it is 125; a 40% fall from that is 125 × 0.6 = 75. The overall change is a decrease of 25%. Simply subtracting the percentages (25 − 40 = -15) is wrong because the second percentage applies to a different base.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"percentages-and-change","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":18,"subtype":"Interest","difficulty":"hard","stimulus":null,"prompt":"£8,000 is invested at 4% interest per year, with the interest added to the balance each year. What is the balance after 2 years?","options":["£8,652.80","£8,320","£8,640","£8,972.80"],"correct_answer":"£8,652.80","explanation":"Each year the balance is multiplied by 1.04: £8,000 × 1.04 × 1.04 = £8,652.80. Simple interest (£8,640) ignores the interest earned on the first year's interest.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"percentages-and-change","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":19,"subtype":"Interest","difficulty":"hard","stimulus":null,"prompt":"£1,000 is invested at 4% interest per year, with the interest added to the balance each year. What is the balance after 2 years?","options":["£1,080","£1,081.60","£1,040","£1,121.60"],"correct_answer":"£1,081.60","explanation":"Each year the balance is multiplied by 1.04: £1,000 × 1.04 × 1.04 = £1,081.60. Simple interest (£1,080) ignores the interest earned on the first year's interest.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"ratios-and-rates","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":10,"subtype":"Ratios","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":null,"prompt":"Tamsin Pharma divides a £4,000 bonus pool between three teams in the ratio 1 : 4 : 5. How much does the second team receive?","options":["£1,333.33","£2,000","£1,600","£160"],"correct_answer":"£1,600","explanation":"The ratio has 10 parts in total, so one part is £4,000 ÷ 10 = £400. The second team gets 4 parts: 4 × £400 = £1,600. Sharing equally (£1,333.33) ignores the ratio.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"ratios-and-rates","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":11,"subtype":"Ratios","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":null,"prompt":"Harlow & Finch divides a £4,000 bonus pool between three teams in the ratio 2 : 5 : 3. How much does the third team receive?","options":["£120","£1,333.33","£1,600","£1,200"],"correct_answer":"£1,200","explanation":"The ratio has 10 parts in total, so one part is £4,000 ÷ 10 = £400. The third team gets 3 parts: 3 × £400 = £1,200. Sharing equally (£1,333.33) ignores the ratio.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"ratios-and-rates","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":12,"subtype":"Averages","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":null,"prompt":"A trainee's scores in five assessments were 57, 56, 66, 54, 60. What score does she need in the sixth assessment for her mean score across all six to be exactly 62?","options":["79","84","65.4","62"],"correct_answer":"79","explanation":"Six scores averaging 62 must total 372. The first five total 293, so the sixth must be 372 − 293 = 79. Scoring the target itself (62) would only work if the first five already averaged 62; their mean is 58.6.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"ratios-and-rates","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":13,"subtype":"Averages","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":null,"prompt":"A trainee's scores in five assessments were 78, 77, 67, 75, 77. What score does she need in the sixth assessment for her mean score across all six to be exactly 70?","options":["46","51","70","65.2"],"correct_answer":"46","explanation":"Six scores averaging 70 must total 420. The first five total 374, so the sixth must be 420 − 374 = 46. Scoring the target itself (70) would only work if the first five already averaged 70; their mean is 74.8.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"ratios-and-rates","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":14,"subtype":"Rates","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":null,"prompt":"4 identical machines make 240 solar panels in 3 hours. How many solar panels would 6 of the same machines make in 4 hours?","options":["480","600","360","320"],"correct_answer":"480","explanation":"One machine makes 240 ÷ 4 ÷ 3 = 20 an hour. 6 machines for 4 hours: 6 × 4 × 20 = 480. Scaling by only the machines or only the hours (360, 320) misses the other factor.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"ratios-and-rates","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":15,"subtype":"Rates","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":null,"prompt":"4 identical machines make 360 storage boxes in 3 hours. How many storage boxes would 6 of the same machines make in 7 hours?","options":["840","540","1,440","1,260"],"correct_answer":"1,260","explanation":"One machine makes 360 ÷ 4 ÷ 3 = 30 an hour. 6 machines for 7 hours: 6 × 7 × 30 = 1,260. Scaling by only the machines or only the hours (540, 840) misses the other factor.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"ratios-and-rates","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":16,"subtype":"Currency","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":null,"prompt":"The exchange rate is £1 = 1.40 euros. A supplier's invoice is for €1,400. What is that in pounds?","options":["£1,398.60","£1,960","£714.29","£1,000"],"correct_answer":"£1,000","explanation":"Divide by the rate: €1,400 ÷ 1.40 = £1,000. Multiplying (£1,960) converts in the wrong direction.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"ratios-and-rates","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":17,"subtype":"Currency","difficulty":"easy","stimulus":null,"prompt":"The exchange rate is £1 = 1.50 Swiss francs. How many Swiss francs do you get for £1,000?","options":["CHF 666.67","CHF 1,500","CHF 15,000","CHF 1,001.50"],"correct_answer":"CHF 1,500","explanation":"Multiply pounds by the rate: £1,000 × 1.50 = CHF 1,500. Dividing (CHF 666.67) would be right for converting the other way.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"ratios-and-rates","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":18,"subtype":"Rates","difficulty":"hard","stimulus":null,"prompt":"A delivery van drives 60 miles to a depot at an average of 30 mph, and returns the same 60 miles at an average of 60 mph. What is its average speed for the whole round trip?","options":["40 mph","45 mph","47 mph","36 mph"],"correct_answer":"40 mph","explanation":"Average speed is total distance ÷ total time. The trip takes 60/30 + 60/60 = 3 hours for 120 miles, so 120 ÷ 3 = 40 mph. Averaging the two speeds (45) ignores that more time is spent at the slower speed.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"ratios-and-rates","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":19,"subtype":"Rates","difficulty":"hard","stimulus":null,"prompt":"A delivery van drives 60 miles to a depot at an average of 60 mph, and returns the same 60 miles at an average of 40 mph. What is its average speed for the whole round trip?","options":["48 mph","50 mph","53 mph","52 mph"],"correct_answer":"48 mph","explanation":"Average speed is total distance ÷ total time. The trip takes 60/60 + 60/40 = 2.5 hours for 120 miles, so 120 ÷ 2.5 = 48 mph. Averaging the two speeds (50) ignores that more time is spent at the slower speed.","exhibit":null,"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"charts-and-tables","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":10,"subtype":"Chart reading","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":null,"prompt":"What was the percentage change in lights sales from last year to this year?","options":["+16.7%","-20%","+20%","+30%"],"correct_answer":"+20%","explanation":"Lights moved from 20 to 24, a change of 4. Divided by last year's 20 that is +20%. Dividing by this year's figure is the usual mistake (+16.7%).","exhibit":{"kind":"bar_chart","caption":"Marlowe Energy: units sold by product (thousands)","unit":"Units sold (thousands)","categories":["Lights","Bottles","Bags","Bikes","Pumps"],"series":[{"name":"Last year","values":[20,60,30,20,40]},{"name":"This year","values":[24,48,45,18,36]}]},"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"charts-and-tables","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":11,"subtype":"Chart reading","difficulty":"easy","stimulus":null,"prompt":"How many pumps (in thousands) were sold across the two years combined?","options":["90","150","60","30"],"correct_answer":"150","explanation":"Add the two bars for Pumps: 60 + 90 = 150 thousand.","exhibit":{"kind":"bar_chart","caption":"Kestrel Components: units sold by product (thousands)","unit":"Units sold (thousands)","categories":["Helmets","Bikes","Pumps","Lights","Locks"],"series":[{"name":"Last year","values":[60,30,60,80,20]},{"name":"This year","values":[90,27,90,72,25]}]},"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"charts-and-tables","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":12,"subtype":"Chart reading","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":null,"prompt":"Which product had the largest increase in units sold between the two years, and by how many thousand?","options":["Locks: +40","Bikes: +20","Locks: +120","Locks: +50"],"correct_answer":"Locks: +40","explanation":"The change for each product is (this year − last year): Bottles +12, Locks +40, Bikes +20, Lights +8, Helmets +8. The largest rise is Locks, +40 thousand.","exhibit":{"kind":"bar_chart","caption":"Lindqvist Textiles: units sold by product (thousands)","unit":"Units sold (thousands)","categories":["Bottles","Locks","Bikes","Lights","Helmets"],"series":[{"name":"Last year","values":[60,80,40,80,80]},{"name":"This year","values":[72,120,60,88,88]}]},"option_figures":null},{"pack_slug":"charts-and-tables","type_slug":"numerical-reasoning","display_order":13,"subtype":"Chart reading","difficulty":"medium","stimulus":null,"prompt":"What was the percentage change in helmet